  <section id="chto-eto">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>The ulnar nerve runs along the inner side of the elbow, in a groove behind the bony bump — the "funny bone" that sends an electric jolt into the little finger when knocked. Here the nerve lies in a narrow canal covered by a ligament. When the arm bends, the canal narrows and the nerve is stretched.</p>
    <p>If the elbow is bent for long periods or you often lean on it, the nerve is constantly compressed — this is how cubital tunnel syndrome develops.</p>
    <NerveMap limb="arm" focus="cubital" lang="en" />
    <h3>Risk factors</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>leaning on the elbow — at a desk, on a car armrest;</li>
      <li>long phone calls, work with a bent arm;</li>
      <li>sleeping with bent arms;</li>
      <li>old fractures and arthritis of the elbow;</li>
      <li>the nerve slipping out of its groove when the elbow bends.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="simptomy">
    <h2>Symptoms</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>numbness and tingling in the little finger and half of the ring finger, including the back of the hand;</li>
      <li>symptoms worsen when the arm is bent for long and at night;</li>
      <li>clumsy fingers: hard to do up a button, open a jar, hold an object;</li>
      <li>later — wasting of the muscle between the thumb and index finger, fingers start to "claw".</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnostika">
    <h2>Diagnosis</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Examination</strong> — area of numbness, strength of small hand muscles, tapping over the nerve at the elbow.</li>
      <li><strong>Nerve conduction study</strong> — shows slowing along the ulnar nerve exactly at the elbow and how much it has suffered.</li>
      <li><strong>Nerve ultrasound</strong> — nerve swelling, its displacement when the elbow bends, cysts and bony spurs.</li>
      <li><strong>Elbow X-ray</strong> — after injuries and with arthritis.</li>
      <li>If in doubt — <strong>cervical spine MRI</strong> to rule out a disc herniation.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="lechenie">
    <h2>Treatment</h2>
    <h3>Without surgery</h3>
    <p>For mild symptoms: do not lean on the elbow, do not keep the arm bent for long, use a headset for the phone. At night — a soft splint or pad that prevents strong elbow flexion. Medication, if needed, is prescribed by a doctor. Five <a href="/en/treatment/nerves/carpal-tunnel-syndrome/#uprazhneniya">hand exercises</a> are in the carpal tunnel article.</p>
    <h3>Surgery</h3>
    <p>Surgery is considered if symptoms do not settle over several months, weakness or muscle wasting appears, or nerve conduction studies show severe compression.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Nerve decompression</strong> — through an incision on the inner elbow the ligament over the canal is divided and the nerve is freed along the whole compressed segment. This is the main option.</li>
      <li><strong>Transposition</strong> — the nerve is also moved forward if it slips out of the groove or the canal is deformed after injury.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>I usually perform the operation under local anaesthesia.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="posle">
    <h2>After surgery</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>You can move your fingers straight away. For the first days the arm is spared from load.</li>
      <li>Stitches are removed on day 10–14, usually by a surgeon near your home.</li>
      <li>Night symptoms often ease in the first days; sensation and strength come back gradually over weeks and months.</li>
      <li>The habit of leaning on the elbow is best dropped for good.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="srochno" slot="urgent">
    <h2>When to seek urgent help</h2>
    <div class="callout alert">
      <p>Sudden weakness of the arm together with weakness of the leg on the same side, a drooping face, speech problems — <strong>call emergency services immediately (112; 103 in Russia).</strong> If numbness appears after an elbow injury or a cut — go to an emergency department the same day.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
